\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
\vspace{-14pt}

A target-swap ablation confirms that KL-discovered mask patterns (68\% divergent from magnitude) are the proximate driver of inference-stage quality.
A predictor ($<$1\% of base) reduces TEAL's perplexity by 18\% at 30\%, renders compensation redundant, and delivers $7.5\times$ smaller cross-seed $\sigma$ under uniform allocation.
Mistral-7B confirms the pattern (5/6 tasks); 14B reveals task-and-scale-dependent effects, motivating N:M sparsity and layer-wise probing.

\paragraph{Cross-Architecture and Scale.}
\label{sec:cross_arch}
On Mistral-7B-v0.3~\cite{jiang2023mistral}, KL outperforms TEAL on 5 of 6 downstream
tasks at 30\% (HellaSwag +8.8pp, ARC +9.0pp, WG +5.6pp, PIQA +3.1pp, MMLU +0.6pp),
with PPL 6.77 vs.\ 8.38; GSM8K is the sole reversal (11.1\% vs.\ 15.9\%,
Table~\ref{tab:mistral_downstream}); the 50\% capacity cliff also holds cross-architecture (MMLU 26.4\%$\approx$random; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_arch}). % exp_id: mistral_kl30_downstream, mistral_teal30_downstream, mistral_teal50_downstream
On Qwen-2.5-14B~\cite{qwen2024qwen25}, KL's PPL advantage generalizes at 30\% ($1.52\times$ dense), but task-level effects diverge: commonsense improves (WG +3.0pp uniform) while factual retrieval (MMLU $-$2.79pp, 3-seed; 63.6\% vs.\ 66.3\%) and reasoning (GSM8K $-$25pp; 32.1$\pm$5.2\% vs.\ 57.1\%, 3-seed) decline (Table~\ref{tab:14b}; actual sparsity: KL 29.6\%, TEAL 26.8\%). Under global allocation, MMLU narrows to $-$8.1pp and GSM8K equalizes (48.8\% vs.\ 49.5\%; \S\ref{sec:scaling}). % exp_id: qwen14b_kl30_uniform_4task, qwen14b_teal30_uniform
These reversals are scale-dependent: at 8B all methods collapse to $<$9\% GSM8K (Table~\ref{tab:main}); at 14B, TEAL retains 65\% of dense reasoning while KL retains 37\% ($\sigma$=5.2pp)---forward KL's mass-covering property disrupts narrow reasoning pathways only when base capacity suffices.
Per-layer Jaccard variance is $2.4\times$ higher at 14B (std 0.076 vs.\ 0.031) with an early-layer convergence zone (L3--L6) absent in 8B; causal linkage requires layer-wise probing. % exp_id: qwen14b_kl30_uniform_4task, qwen14b_teal30_uniform % exp_id: qwen14b_kl30_s42, qwen14b_teal30_eval, qwen14b_bce30_s42_eval, qwen14b_kl30_uniform_4task, qwen14b_teal30_uniform_4task
\paragraph{Inference Efficiency.}
\label{sec:overhead}
The predictor adds 3.17\,ms (2.7\% overhead, 75.96M params / 0.9\% of base); actual speedup requires sparse kernel support (Appendix~\ref{app:latency}). % exp_id: latency_profiling

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

\paragraph{Model and Predictor.}
We evaluate on LLaMA-3.1-8B~\cite{grattafiori2024llama3}, which uses SwiGLU FFN blocks~\cite{shazeer2020glu} with $d_{\text{model}}=4096$ and $d_{\text{ff}}=14336$ across 32 layers.
The sparsity predictor is a two-layer MLP with 2.37M parameters per layer (75.9M total), implemented as Linear($4096 \to 128$) + GELU + Linear($128 \to 14336$), trained to produce per-neuron importance scores that minimize output-distribution divergence under sparsity.

\paragraph{Training.}
All predictors are trained for 1000 steps on WikiText-103 with sequence length 2048 and batch size 4 on a single NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Server Edition GPU (96\,GB), using AdamW~\cite{loshchilov2019adamw} with learning rate $1 \times 10^{-3}$, weight decay $5 \times 10^{-2}$, and cosine decay.
The KL predictor uses Gumbel-Sigmoid relaxation~\cite{jang2017gumbel} with temperature $\tau=1.0$ annealed to $0.1$ over training.

\paragraph{Evaluation.}
We report perplexity on WikiText-2~\cite{merity2017pointer} (seq\_len=2048) and downstream accuracy on ARC-Challenge (25-shot), WinoGrande (5-shot), MMLU (5-shot), GSM8K (8-shot), HellaSwag (10-shot), and PIQA (0-shot).

\paragraph{Inference Protocol.}
At inference, the predictor produces per-neuron scores that are passed to TEAL's global top-$k$ allocation mechanism~\cite{liu2025teal}, which distributes the sparsity budget across layers non-uniformly.
We term this \textit{predictor-TEAL}: the predictor replaces TEAL's magnitude-based scoring while retaining its allocation strategy.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
We compare against: (1) \textbf{Vanilla TEAL}~\cite{liu2025teal}, which uses activation magnitudes directly\footnote{TEAL is deterministic; single-run evaluation suffices. KL/BCE predictors require multi-seed evaluation due to random initialization.}; (2) \textbf{BCE predictor} (DejaVu-style~\cite{liu2023dejavu}), trained with per-neuron binary cross-entropy on magnitude-based oracle masks for 1000 and 2700 steps; and (3) \textbf{Uniform allocation}, which distributes the sparsity budget equally across layers.


\subsection{Main Results}

Table~\ref{tab:main} presents the primary comparison. Uniform allocation preserves downstream performance best; global allocation is analyzed to understand allocation-quality tradeoffs (\S\ref{sec:scaling}).
At 30\% sparsity, KL distillation under uniform allocation achieves PPL 8.81$\pm$0.02 (3-seed), reducing TEAL's perplexity by 18\% (10.82 $\to$ 8.81). % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform, teal30_uniform
HellaSwag (86.0\% retention) and PIQA (92.5\% retention) confirm moderate degradation on non-reasoning tasks (Appendix~\ref{app:downstream}). % exp_id: hellaswag_piqa_kl30_uniform
Uniform allocation at 30\% achieves PPL 8.81$\pm$0.02 with stable downstream performance (MMLU 40.7$\pm$1.3\%, 3-seed; full stability analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:stability}), while global top-$k$ exhibits high seed variance (35.7$\pm$9.5\%) from late-layer starvation (L31 retention 28.4\%) interacting with predictor initialization (\S\ref{sec:scaling}). % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform, kl30_s123_uniform, kl30_s456_uniform, kl30_3seed

At 50\% sparsity, the PPL advantage widens: 11.79 vs.\ 23.31 (49\% reduction over TEAL). % exp_id: bench_kl50_global, bench_teal50
The predictor generalizes to C4 validation at both 30\% (PPL 16.03$\pm$0.01, 2-seed) and 50\% (52.5\% over TEAL; Appendix~\ref{app:ood}).
However, all methods exhibit downstream collapse on MMLU (28.1$\pm$0.2\% vs.\ 28.0$\pm$0.6\%, both $\approx$ random, 3-seed) and GSM8K ($<$2.2\%); this is a capacity-limited regime where no allocation strategy can recover. % exp_id: bench_kl50_global, d030_kl50_uniform, multi_seed_kl50_global

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Main results on LLaMA-3.1-8B (WikiText-2 PPL$\downarrow$; downstream accuracy$\uparrow$). \textbf{Bold}: best per sparsity. $^\ast$Extended training budget ($2.71\times$ KL step budget), no compensation; training details in \S\ref{sec:isocompute}. $^\dagger$BCE 30\%: TEAL global (2700 steps). $\pm$: 3-seed mean$\pm$std ($s \in \{42, 123, 456\}$); unmarked: seed 42.}
\label{tab:main}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{llccccc}
\toprule
Sparsity & Method & PPL$\downarrow$ & ARC-c & WG & MMLU & GSM8K \\
\midrule
0\% & Dense & 6.12 & 58.28 & 78.06 & 65.27 & 48.75 \\
\midrule
\multirow{4}{*}{30\%}
& TEAL uniform & 10.82 & 39.33 & 65.04 & \textbf{45.86} & \textbf{8.79} \\ % exp_id: teal30_uniform
& KL global (Ours) & 8.96$\pm$0.02 & \textbf{43.8$\pm$3.6} & \textbf{70.2$\pm$0.9} & 35.7$\pm$9.5 & 8.0$\pm$0.7 \\ % exp_id: kl30_3seed_global
& KL uniform & \textbf{8.81$\pm$0.02} & 43.7$\pm$2.5 & 68.9$\pm$0.4 & 40.7$\pm$1.3 & 7.5$\pm$0.1 \\ % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform, kl30_s123_uniform, kl30_s456_uniform

